		(property "Wright.PickReason" "2026-11-05 Fuse x1: needs I rated >= 34.7A, V rated >= 72V. jlcparts Active (catalog status field (JLC/LCSC listing, not the manufacturer)), stock 777, 2.4374 USD each at 1. I rated 40A meets min 34.7A; V rated 72V meets min 72V. Design-in: no open design in the corpus uses it. Freshness: catalogue freshness 0.00 (C720195 is in the newest 15% of 1,533 listings in Disposable Fuses + Automotive Fuses + Fuses). (15 other candidates passed; 6 other candidates on the shortlist) Chosen because: Replaces the turn-1 S1032-F-60.0A pick (and the refused Eaton 1025HC40-RTR, no catalogue listing). Constraints re-derived with wright parts constraints --kind fuse --i-cont 26 --vbus-max 43.2: the spec's 40 A is the fuse RATING (block purpose: 40 A / 72 VDC fuse; pads sized for the 40 A fuse rating), not the load; the continuous bus current is bounded by 2 channels x ~13 A continuous phase current = 26 A (bus current cannot exceed phase current at under 100 percent duty), so the 75 percent derate rule asks I rated >= 34.7 A and a 40 A fuse meets it (26/40 = 65 percent); turn 1 had fed the 40 A rating in as the load, which produced the 60 A pick. V rated >= 72 V is the spec's 72 VDC line (1.67x the 43.2 V pack). Littelfuse 0456040.DR (NANO2 456 series, very fast acting) read from the Littelfuse 456 datasheet rev 11/22/19 page 1 and 3: 40 A, 72 V max, interrupting 180 A at 72 VDC and 600 A at 60 VDC (the pack maximum is 43.2 V, so the 600 A figure applies), cold resistance 1.05 mohm, melting I2t 191 A2s, voltage drop 55 mV, 100 percent holds 4 h min, 200 percent opens within 60 s; body 12.50 x 4.445 mm; recommended pad layout for the 40 A size = 3.79 x 5.65 mm pads, 5.72 mm gap, 13.3 mm overall, which is exactly the Eaton 1025HC land pattern already on the board (Wright:Fuse_Eaton_1025HC_40-50A_L12.4mm_W4.5mm), so no footprint or placement change; datasheet asks for >= 15 mm trace width at 100 um copper for the 40 A size, to be checked in power-path. Over S1032-F-40.0A (SART, 40 A, 3473 in stock, 1.33 USD) because Littelfuse is a preferred maker with UL 248-14 recognition, its interrupt rating at the pack voltage is 600 A against SART's 180 A (an A123 12S pack can source far more than 180 A into a short; the fault current is not given, so --i-fault stays an open question), and its land pattern matches the existing footprint while the S1032 would need a new 10.25 x 3.2 mm one. Over the 60 A S1032-F-60.0A because 60 A exceeds the 40 A the spec names and protects less. Over Littelfuse 142.5631.xxxx bolt-mount parts because they are not SMD and rated 58 VDC. Over the JFC1032 parts because they have no datasheet URL. 777 in stock covers 1 at 2.44 USD. Lifecycle basis is the JLC/LCSC catalogue only (Digi-Key/Mouser keys off), so the manufacturer status is not checked; fuses are age-exempt. Fuse time-current curve against two simultaneous 30 A phase bursts is not checked: not a current qualification. Gate: lifecycle Unknown: jlcparts listing lists it, which is a catalogue listing, not a manufacturer status; no manufacturer lifecycle source is configured (no Digi-Key or Mouser key in parts.json), so the gate cannot prove Active; introduction date unknown (no datasheet first date, research report or curated row), so the age rule could not run"
			(at 101.6 76.2 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
