			)
		)
		(property "Manufacturer" "STMicroelectronics"
			(at 350.52 149.86 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
		(property "Wright.PickReason" "2026-11-03 Any part (keyword only) x1: needs no electrical constraint. jlcparts Active (catalog status field (JLC/LCSC listing, not the manufacturer)), stock 140, 13.6488 USD each at 1.  (0 other candidates passed) Chosen because: Motor MCU, STM32G4 family in LQFP-64 as required. Constraints derived before searching: two advanced-control timers (TIM1 and TIM8, each with 3 complementary channel pairs, dead-time and BKIN) for two 3-phase half-bridge sets; six simultaneous low-side current channels plus VBUS; LQFP-64; 170 MHz; headroom for dual-motor FOC plus a BLE UART protocol (the firmware size is NOT given, so flash is a judgment, not a derived number). STM32G474RET6: 512 KB flash, 128 KB RAM, TIM1/TIM8/TIM20, five 12-bit ADCs so each motor gets its own ADC pair (ADC1/2 for motor 1, ADC3/4 for motor 2) with injected sampling off its own timer, CORDIC and FMAC, 15 May 2019 first release (age about 7 years). The datasheet AF table shows TIM1/TIM8 CH1-3, CH1N-3N and BKIN pins on LQFP64 (PA6, PA7, PB0, PB1, PB12-15, PC6-8, PA8-10); the exact assignment that avoids the PA7/PB0/PB1 overlap between the two timers is left to the schematic phase and CubeMX. Alternative kept for fallback: pin-compatible STM32G431RBT6 (also TIM1 + TIM8, only 128 KB flash / 32 KB RAM, two ADCs shared by both motors; JLC 2,845 in stock, 5.78 USD), used in the prior art below. SUPPLY RISK, stated plainly: Digi-Key.ca shows Active but 0 in stock and a 52-week lead time; JLC has 140 (covers this board); ST's own page was not read, so lifecycle evidence is a distributor page (research/STM32G474RET6.json), not a manufacturer statement, and no successor notice was seen (Digi-Key lists only an Infineon XMC4400 as 'Similar'). If the build is larger than a few boards, move to the STM32G431RBT6. Prior art: memirmakar/foc-driver BOM_revB.csv uses STM32G431RBT6 (LQFP-64) with a 24 MHz crystal, and its HANDOVER.md sets BOOT0 through the nSWBOOT0 option byte; it is a small two-commit project, so a design reference, not proof."
			(at 350.52 149.86 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
		(property "Wright.Source" "jlcparts C521608"
